+# Claim audit, 2026-10-20
+
+Independent results were saved in `replication2.md` at 19:04:33 UTC before opening the revised summary, findings, analysis script or Agy report. `audit2.py` subsequently compared the published CSVs and recomputed supplementary claims from raw inputs. `comparison_checks.json` records 8,542 matching numeric cells, with maximum absolute difference below 3e-14. The rendered summary tables were checked against those CSVs. Both chart images were inspected; the entire 950-row NADAC chart series also reproduces.
+
+| Write-up location | Check and disposition |
+| --- | --- |
+| Introduction, lines 7–15 | Raw selected families number 10, 15 and 15. First implementation is 2026-01-01; the next 15 have MFPs from 2027-01-01. The 2028 cohort has no published MFP values in the supplied file. [CMS selection and price documentation](https://www.cms.gov/initiatives/medicare-prescription-drug-affordability/overview/medicare-drug-price-negotiation-program/selected-drugs-negotiated-prices) supports the cohort chronology. These are descriptive comparison cohorts, not validated untreated controls. |
+| Finding 1, numerical results | Median -63.688486%; family range -30.424101% to -70.780387%; 10/10 families fall at least 30%. Fixed-weight changes: -61.374450%, +1.482469%, +1.225234%, +1.082060%. Other brands: 741 products, median +0.921902%, quartiles -2.047596% and +3.567191%; 13/741 = 1.754386% fall at least 30%. Linzess falls 49.018637%, with a WAC-related NADAC cut of 50.27% effective 2026-01-01. All published rounded numbers match. |
+| Finding 1, headline and pooled insulin family | “About two thirds for all ten” overstates individual declines. NovoLog/Fiasp is a combined selected family. Separately, named NovoLog products fall 8.778743% and Fiasp products 72.030372%, while the combined family falls 30.424101%. The “every one” statement is true at the defined family level, not for every constituent brand. See `insulin_split_partd.csv`. |
+| Finding 1, explanation of other declines | Eleven of the 13 products belong to the named insulin and empagliflozin-combination sets. The remaining two are Yuflyma(Cf) Autoinjector (-42.0%) and Butalbital-Acetaminophen-Caffe (-30.7%). The study does not establish simultaneous WAC cuts for those two. Replace the universal explanation with “11 of the 13.” Raw comparison data support the named WAC events, but not causal attribution of the Part D changes. |
+| Finding 1, payment explanation | The MFP-plus-dispensing-fee ceiling follows [Pub. L. 117-169, amendment to SSA §1860D-2(d)(1)(D)](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-117publ169/html/PLAW-117publ169.htm). Gross PDE amounts can therefore reflect MFP-based payment. The text correctly stops short of asserting universal claim compliance or estimating the causal contribution. |
+| Finding 2, cuts and product identity | All percentages, dates and identities reproduce: Eliquis -43%, Jardiance -44.39%, Farxiga -37%, Fiasp -75% on 2026-01-01; NovoLog -75% on 2024-01-01; Januvia/Janumet -42.41% on 2025-01-01. January 2026 counts are 21/69 selected NDCs and 33/2,198 other NDCs. The latter comprise 16 Synjardy, 8 Trijardy, 6 Glyxambi and 3 Tresiba NDCs. February adds 4 Lyumjev and 1 Humalog NDC, each -70% on 2026-02-01. Product descriptions identify the Humalog concentration as 200 units/mL. |
+| Finding 2, increases and interpretation | Expanded-window increases are 32/69 = 46.376812% versus 863/2,198 = 39.262966%. Selected increases: 19 Xarelto NDCs on February 3; 4 Enbrel and 7 Entresto on January 7; 2 Stelara on January 2. “As common” is acceptable as an informal magnitude comparison, not an equivalence test. The shared manufacturer/ingredient clustering is descriptive. The [Medicaid manufacturer release](https://www.medicaid.gov/prescription-drugs/downloads/mfr-rel-119.pdf) confirms the 2024-01-01 rebate-cap removal; it does not identify the motive behind any specific cut. |
+| Finding 3, ratios | Every displayed ratio matches. December range excluding NovoLog: 2.156078–3.991037, displayed 2.2–4.0. The four increasing ratios rise 2.20%–5.06%, consistent with the rounded 2%–5% statement. The raw CMS rows verify the June 1 Entresto Sprinkle MFP change. No Imbruvica match occurs anywhere in the supplied 2024–2026 NADAC files. |
+| Finding 3, sensitivity wording | All-NDC sensitivities reproduce, including generic-rate insulin aspart and dapagliflozin. “Reported separately” refers to additional all-NDC columns, not a generic-only estimate. Constant-panel Entresto is 2.217951 versus cross-sectional 2.215230 in October: both display as 2.22. Replace “are the same” with “agree at the displayed precision.” Other families match exactly. |
+| Finding 3, invoice gap and refunds | The national-list-cut-only assertion is unsupported. [NADAC methodology, p. 23](https://www.medicaid.gov/medicaid-chip-program-information/by-topics/prescription-drugs/ful-nadac-downloads/nadacmethodology.pdf) also permits brand-rate changes from monthly invoice surveys and substantiated Help Desk reviews. An off-invoice refund is not directly reflected in the original invoice. [CMS effectuation guidance, §§40.4–40.4.4](https://www.cms.gov/files/document/ipay-2028-final-guidance.pdf) supports prospective access and retrospective refunds, generally using WAC minus MFP; the benchmark gap is not a measured pharmacy receivable. |
+| Limit: gross, not net | Gross cost, dispensing fees, aggregation across strengths and exclusion of manufacturer rebates are supported by the [quarterly methodology](https://data.cms.gov/sites/default/files/2025-12/QDD_PTD_RQ2503_Methodology_508.pdf). The 22% figure is correctly described as a 2023 counterfactual in the [CMS fact sheet](https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/fact-sheets/medicare-drug-price-negotiation-program-negotiated-prices-initial-price-applicability-year-2026). No observed net savings are estimated. Drug-specific historical confidential rebate amounts are not verified by these files. |
+| Limit: preliminary quarter | The current [CMS dataset page](https://data.cms.gov/summary-statistics-on-use-and-payments/medicare-medicaid-spending-by-drug/medicare-quarterly-part-d-spending-by-drug) states three-month extraction beginning July 2026 and cautions about revisions. The older methodology PDF says six months and should not be used for the current timing. |
+| Limit: within-family mix | Annualized claim changes reproduce: Entresto -86.496357%, Stelara -43.774078%, Farxiga -30.451799%. [Novartis's 2025 report](https://www.novartis.com/sites/novartiscom/files/novartis-20-f-2025.pdf) dates US Entresto generic entry to Q3 2025; [Fresenius's launch announcement](https://www.fresenius-kabi.com/us/news-and-events/fresenius-continues-growth-of-biosimilars-portfolio-with-the-us-availability-of-otulfi-ustekinumab-aauz) confirms a US ustekinumab biosimilar launch in March 2025. “May reflect” is appropriately conditional. |
+| Limit: between-product mix | Other-brand pooled change is +10.857537%, fixed-weight change +1.082060%. Their difference, 9.775477 percentage points, equals the independently computed contribution from changing claim shares at 2026 per-claim values. Largest positive contributors include Mounjaro, Jascayd, Dupixent Pen, Zepbound and Nubeqa, supporting the GLP-1/specialty examples. This identity is an accounting decomposition; it does not identify why claim shares changed. Within-product strength/form/days-supply effects remain. |
+| Limit: name-based groups | Revised filtering reproduces exactly for the observed data, including residual generics. NovoLog Mix is excluded from selected mapping, but both NovoLog Mix 70-30 and its FlexPen row remain among the 741 Other brands and pass the volume threshold. Their 2025 claims are 33,025 and 178,837. Clarify this scope rather than implying exclusion from the analysis. |
+| Limit: NADAC scope, units, outliers | Brand counts are exactly 69 and 70. Unweighted NDC medians and rate grouping are correctly stated. Both systems use EA/ML/GM conventions; the [CMS final guidance, p. 368](https://www.cms.gov/files/document/ipay-2028-final-guidance.pdf) describes the billing standard. The supplied MFP file lacks a unit field for independent per-NDC concordance. Fiasp PumpCart's MFP is 6.956927 per unit, equal to the vial value; its latest ratio is 1.232579. The two named anomaly types reproduce, but no exhaustive externally validated unit audit is implied. |
+| Limit: deselection | Raw effective/end dates and remarks identify Entresto, NovoLog/Fiasp, Stelara and Xarelto as deselected from 2027. Six original families retain valid rows on 2027-01-01: Eliquis, Enbrel, Farxiga, Imbruvica, Januvia and Jardiance. The current [CMS deselection table](https://www.cms.gov/initiatives/medicare-prescription-drug-affordability/overview/medicare-drug-price-negotiation-program/selected-drugs-negotiated-prices) corroborates all four. |
+| Limit: noncausal design | Appropriate. Neither causality nor patient out-of-pocket spending is estimated. Later cohorts, related products, generic entry, benefit design and possible anticipation remain plausible competing explanations. |
+| Future work, items 1–4 | Appropriate proposed extensions, not completed analyses. Matched earlier quarters would test seasonality. Per-unit measures remove the direct quantity-per-claim component but still mix strengths/forms. Prospective 2027 outcomes should acknowledge the already-seen 2026 data. Pharmacy exposure requires quantities, actual prices, timing and eligibility/340B handling. No release date or actual exposure amount is verified or asserted. |
+| Provenance/status and charts | Prior verdict labels are accurate, but “both reviews' fixes are applied” should be qualified until the wording corrections in this follow-up are made. Figure values reproduce. Prefer a descriptive Part D chart title that does not imply causal attribution. The latest NADAC date is 2026-10-21 as supplied, one day after retrieval; no assertion that it was observed on October 20 is warranted. |
+
+Two implementation distinctions do not change these published numbers. The study script uses the last day of February, hence February 29 in 2024, despite the February 28 label. This replication uses February 28 literally. For pre-2026 snapshots, the script requires an interval covering January 1, whereas the task says to use each NDC's earliest 2026 row; the snapshot matches are unchanged here.
+
+**Shortcuts taken:** current extraction timing was checked through indexed text from the CMS dataset page because direct retrieval returned 403. No external commercial NDC billing-unit crosswalk or claim-level payment/refund data was obtained; this review verifies aggregate arithmetic and the stated source conventions, not individual transactions.
